          %h2#Influences Influences
          %blockquote
            %p
              The importance of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
              for Hegel should not be underestimated.
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} Geometry
              was among his earliest intellectual interests (cf. Jaeschke , Hegel-Handbuch, 1) and he devoted intensive study to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+geometry"} Euclid 's Elements
              in the period leading up to his move from Frankfurt to Jena (cf. Johannes Hoffmeister[ed.], Dokumente zu Hegel’s Entwicklung [Stuttgart-Bad Cannstatt : Fromann 1936], 288–300)… In 1805/6 he taught courses in arithmetic and geometry both in Jena and at the Nuremberg Gymnasium while he was working on the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Science+of+Logic"} Science of Logic
          %h2#PerceptionOfHegelsPhilosophy Perception of Hegel’s Naturphilosophie
          %p
            Some comments on the perception of Hegel’s
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metaphysics"} metaphysics
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Redding12"} Redding 12
          %p
            Hegel’s
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+philosophy"} natural philosophy
            as laid out in particular in his
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Science+of+Logic"} Science of Logic
            may easily seem rather mysterious and was accordingly criticized. Being partly a creation myth in the spirit of
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heraclitus"} Heraclitus
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logos"} logos
            and being all thoroughly arranged in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unity+of+opposites"} unity of opposites
            that taken at face value are typically plain self-contradictions, to a large extent it seems more a work of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mysticism"} mysticism
            than of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/philosophy"} philosophy
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/science"} science
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/becoming"} becoming
            of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nothing"} nothing
            into
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/being"} being
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+philosophy"} natural philosophy
          %p
            In reaction to this, the rise of the school of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+philosophy"} analytic philosophy
            in England defined itself in its opposition to, and its rejection of, Hegel’s
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/philosophy"} philosophy
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            and the bolstering of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic"} intuitionistic logic
            via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            in the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations+of+mathematics"} foundations of mathematics
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"} William Lawvere
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unity+of+opposites"} unity of opposites
            may usefully be formalized as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+pairs"} adjoint pairs
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+monad"} idempotent
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonad"}> co
            \-)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads"}> monads
            \/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modalities"} modalities
            in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+being"} category of being
            and see at
            %em
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Science%20of%20Logic#formalization_dictionary"} Science of Logic – Formalization dictionary
            for an idea of how such a dictionary can be used to read Hegel’s natural philosophy as being a kind of pseudocode for an actual axiomatization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos+theory"} topos theory
          %p The opposition against Hegel’s philosophy is expressed for instance in
          %ul
            %li John Stuart Mill, letter of November 4, 1867 to Alexander Bain:
          %blockquote
            %p Besides these I have been toiling through Stirling’s “Secret of Hegel”. It is right to learn what Hegel is &amp; one learns it only too well from Stirling’s book. I say “too well” because I found by actual experience of Hegel that conversancy with him tends to deprave one’s intellect. The attempt to unwind an apparently infinite series of self–contradictions, not disguised but openly faced &amp; coined into [illegible word] science by being stamped with a set of big abstract terms, really if persisted in impairs the acquired delicacy of perception of false reasoning &amp; false thinking which has been gained by years of careful mental discipline with terms of real meaning. For some time after I had finished the book all such words as reflexion, development, evolution, &amp;c., gave me a sort of sickening feeling which I have not yet entirely got rid of.
          %p and in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bertrand+Russell"}> Bertrand Russell
              , Chapter XXXI of
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A+History+of+Western+Philosophy"} A History of Western Philosophy
              (1945)
          %blockquote
            %p In philosophy ever since the time of Pythagoras there has been an opposition between the men whose thought was mainly inspired by mathematics and those who were more influenced by the empirical sciences. Plato, Thomas Aquinas, Spinoza, and Kant belong to what may be called the mathematical party; Democritus, Aristotle, and the modern empiricist from Locke onwards, belong to the opposite party. In our day a school of philosophy has arisen which sets to work to eliminate Pythagoreanism from the principles of mathematics, and to combine empiricism with an interest in the deductive parts of human knowledge. The aims of this school are less spectacular than those of most philosophers in the past, but some of its achievements are as solid as those of the men of science.
            %p
              The origin of this philosophy is in the achievements of mathematicians who set to work to purge their subject of fallacies and slipshod reasoning. The great mathematicians of the seventeenth century were optimistic and anxious for quick results; consequently they left the foundations of analytical geometry and the infinitesimal calculus insecure. Leibnitz believed in actual infinitesimals, but although this belief suited his metaphysics it has no sound basis in mathematics. Weierstrass, soon after the middle of the nineteenth century, showed how to establish the calculus without infinitesimals, and thus at last made it logically secure. Next came
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Cantor"} Georg Cantor
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hegel"} Hegel
          %p
            (Here it is worth remarking that not much later
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+objects"} infinitesimal objects
            as anticipated by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gottfried+Leibniz"} Gottfried Leibniz
            were given a neat formal basis in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential geometry
            and
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonstandard+analysis"} nonstandard analysis
          %p and in
          %ul
            %li
              Hans Reichenbach, p. 72 of
              = succeed "," do
                %em The rise of scientific philosophy
          %blockquote
            %p Hegel has been called the successor of Kant; that is a serious misunderstanding of Kant and an unjustified elevation of Hegel. Kant’s system, though proved untenable by later developments, was the attempt of a great mind to establish rationalism on a scientific basis. Hegel’s system is the poor construction of a fanatic who has seen one empirical truth and attempts to make it a logical law within the most unscientific of all logics. Whereas Kant’s system marks the peak of the historical line of rationalism, Hegel’s system belongs in the period of decay of speculative philosophy which characterizes the nineteenth century.
          %p
            The point that Hegel’s controversial views on the foundations of physics were later partly vindicated by modern developments is made by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/R.+G.+Collingwood"} R. G. Collingwood
            in his book
            %em An Essay on Metaphysics
            (1940). He writes concerning the views expressed by the physicist
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Arthur+Eddington"} Arthur Eddington
            in the 1928 Gifford lectures:
          %blockquote
            %p
              The discovery by a very distinguished scientist that there are grains of sense in Hegel’s
              = succeed "," do
                %em Naturphilosophie
          %p
            This refers to Hegel’s complaint about
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Newton%27s+laws+of+motion"} Newton's laws of motion
            which give the picture of the planets kept in their orbits by the operation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forces"} forces
            “like stupid children pushed and pulled into place at drill”, while rather they ought to be thought of as moving freely and Eddington’s suggestion that according to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein%27s+laws+of+motion"} Einstein's laws of motion
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
            this idea of “free” motion may indeed be argued to be closer to the physical truth.
          %p Another place where Hegel is seen to have have expressed a vision of insights scientifically available only in a distant future is in
          %ul
            %li
              Slavoj Zizek,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://bigthink.com/videos/we-need-thinking"} We need thinking
          %blockquote
            %p I claim that what is happening, for example, in quantum physics in the last 100 of years – these things which are so daring, incredible, that we cannot include into our conscious view of reality – that Hegel’s philosophy, with all it’s dialectical paradoxes, can be of some help here. I claim that reading quantum physics through Hegel and vice versa is very productive.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Peirce"} Charles Peirce
            had a more ambivalent view of Hegel:
          %blockquote
            %p My three categories are nothing but Hegel’s three grades of thinking. (CP 8.213),
          %p but
          %blockquote
            %p
              A
              %em Phenomenology
              which does not reckon with pure mathematics, a science hardly come to years of discretion when Hegel wrote, will be the same pitiful club-footed affair that Hegel produced. (CP 5.40).
